          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Uncircumcision signifies sinful or resistant flesh, inward hardness and spiritual insensibility, often also marking impurity or exclusion from covenant standing.\",\"book_summary\":\"The foreskin is obstructing flesh covering a covenant member and hindering the proper function of heart, ear, lips, or fruit.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Uncircumcision is resistant, unconsecrated flesh or the resulting spiritual incapacity.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Uncircumcision is flesh functioning as an obstruction to a covered member's proper activity.\",\"core_relation\":\"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book systematizes heart, ear, lips, and fruit as instances of one functional-obstruction pattern and centers the covered covenant member. The consensus more broadly includes moral impurity, sinful nature, and covenant-outside status without requiring literal or functional obstruction in every case.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction: resistant flesh can operate as an obstruction. The material difference is that the book makes obstruction the defining mechanism rather than treating incapacity as one effect among broader meanings such as impurity, rebellion, or outsider status.\",\"rationale\":\"The book retains the consensus's central association with flesh that prevents proper spiritual responsiveness, especially in the uncircumcised heart and ears. It refines the category by extending a single obstruction model to lips and fruit while narrowing attention toward something covering a covenant member, thereby reclassifying cases the consensus may explain through impurity, immaturity, or covenant exclusion.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The cited texts explicitly connect uncircumcision with stubborn hearts, inability to hear, deficient speech, forbidden fruit, and putting off flesh, but they do not uniformly state that physical or metaphorical flesh blocks each object's proper work. Leviticus 19:23 marks fruit as prohibited or unconsecrated, not clearly obstructed from fruitfulness, while several biblical uses denote covenant outsiders rather than covered covenant members.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
